# Concurrency

## Pick the primitive

| Need | Use |
|---|---|
| Hand one result back | `std::sync::mpsc` (`worker::spawn` → `Outcome`) |
| Counters read every frame | `AtomicU64`, `Ordering::Relaxed` |
| Small shared string/struct | `Mutex<T>`, guard held for one statement |
| Many readers, rare writers | `RwLock<T>` |
| Data-parallel per-entry work | `rayon` pool sized by `--threads` (cap 64) |
| Stop a running job | `Arc<AtomicBool>` polled per buffer → return `Error::Interrupted` |

- Shared ownership across threads is `Arc<T>`; `Rc` / `RefCell` never cross a thread.
- Anything crossing threads must be `Send + Sync`. `ProgressSink: Send + Sync` is part of its contract and implementations must be cheap and non-blocking.
- Never hold a `Mutex` guard across I/O, a channel send, or another lock. Lock, copy out, unlock.
- Recover poisoned locks: `lock().unwrap_or_else(|p| p.into_inner())`.
- Prefer moving data through channels over sharing it; use `std::thread::scope` when borrows are enough.

```rust
// ❌ BAD: guard held during I/O
let mut g = state.lock().unwrap();
g.bytes += io::copy(&mut r, &mut w)?;

// ✅ GOOD
let n = io::copy(&mut r, &mut w)?;
state.done.fetch_add(n, Ordering::Relaxed);
```

## GUI thread rule

- The `eframe` update loop only paints and reads snapshots. Archive I/O, directory reads, and listings run through `worker::spawn` and return as `Outcome` messages.
- Tag results with a `generation` counter so a slow job cannot overwrite a newer navigation.

## Parallel compression (ZIP)

- Workers compress entries to temp pieces; one writer merges in input order with `raw_copy_file`. Output bytes must match the single-thread path.
- Bounded in-flight work: memory ≈ `threads × chunk`, never an unbounded queue of finished pieces.

## Deadlocks and races

- Acquire locks in one fixed order; keep critical sections tiny.
- No spin-waits; use channels, `Condvar`, or `recv_timeout`.
- `async` / `await` is not used in this workspace. Do not add an executor for I/O-bound CLI work; threads and channels are sufficient.
